Z||000000||Uaana||04-09-2000||05:39 AM||||Ok I cheated to get your attention but felt this needed saying.<BR>If you look at the pics in the fiero crash pics post you will notice a something that those of us who were/are in the EMS field see<BR>all the time.  Look at the windshield of the silver car.  Both dummies recieved a windshield taste test. Ask a survivor of one, there are a few.. not something you would like to recieve.<BR>I know this is a little long but I get the feeling from time to time that because of the much praised structural saftey of these cars people are taking unneeded risks. So yes your body will be in one piece but some ambulance driver will still have to scrape whats left of your brain out of the windshield if you get in a headon and are not wearing your SeatBelt.<P>I'll step of my soapbox now thank you for your time.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||04-09-2000||08:40 AM||||Seems like the original crash report did mentioned that the Fiero was fairly safe <B>if</B> the seat belts were properly used. It's simple physics, you don't have to be a rocket scientist. Our little Fieros are small and relatively light weight. If you tangle with a full size pickup truck that weighs twice as much, which one do you think will win?||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||04-09-2000||08:42 AM||||Ok, everyone got two safety lectures, now where are those bikini pics???||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-09-2000||10:17 AM||||Here is a link from an NIFE club member who was T-boned by a full size pick-up truck. He was injured less than the driver of the pick-up! (pick-up driver was not wearing his seatbelt.). The 86 SE was cut in half but the guy walked away! (complete with pics)<BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/art-wreck.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/art-wreck.html</A> ||||reg||4
Z||000004||TheFiero64||04-09-2000||10:39 AM||||I wonder why it split in half like that? Good thing there was no passenger. Did you see the passenger seat all mangled on the ground? It is a safe car don't get me wrong, but WOW that thing just split in half! Most compact & sports cars I see that were in side impacts look like this too. I thought the fiero would absorb the impact differently. oh well, It still is safer than most.||||reg||10
Z||000005||Raydar||04-09-2000||02:32 PM||||Uaana,<BR>That's okay. We'll forgive you for the slight deception. I do realize the importance of wearing seatbelts. I used to have an attitude of "I'll do it if I think about it." My long-time-ago G/F's mom used to always "remind" us about wearing seat belts. One day, those seat belts stopped both of us from eating the dashboard of my Monza, when a 76 Grand Prix turned in front of us. (Guess who lost THAT encounter.) I hit the GP doing about 30-35, I guess. Watched my glasses fly straight off my face and hit the windshield. The only injury to either of us were some stiff muscles, and my "barked" shin. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||WisconsinGT||04-09-2000||07:40 PM||||Uaana, you are correct on the seatbelt issue. I am one of the few that survived a head-on with a truck. (I was in the passengers seat of a Chevette) Even though I was wearing my seatbelt, the hood invited itself into the car. From the impact my head wanted to say hello to the windshield. Two months later after awakening from my comma, and back surgery, I was told what happened. Anyway, I really think seatblets are great. But there is a point of no return no matter what vehicle you are in. I do like the fact that I am sitting so far back from the dash that I think it would be hard for the two of us to meet.||||reg||1
